## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to build a scalable, privacy-preserving ML pipeline that composes multiple AWS services (S3, Step Functions, Lambda, SageMaker) with governance and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven pipelines with S3 triggers
- Step Functions coordinating Lambda and SageMaker
- PII redaction in Lambda
- SageMaker inference integration for sentiment and topics
- Data governance: KMS encryption, least-privilege IAM, auditing
- Reliability: DLQ, retries, monitoring, cost controls

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda pseudocode for redacting PII from JSON input
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.body || '{}');
  if (data.email) data.email = '[REDACTED]';
  if (data.phone) data.phone = '[REDACTED]';
  return { redacted: true, data };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate redaction quality and model bias at scale?
- What strategies ensure latency targets under burst traffic?
- How would you enforce data-retention policies and revocation requests across services?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end, privacy-aware, event-driven pipeline on AWS using basic services.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and Lambda integration
- Amazon Comprehend for sentiment, entities, and PII
- Redaction patterns and data privacy controls
- Separate storage for redacted transcripts and analytics
- DynamoDB upsert for fast lookups
- IAM, encryption (SSE-KMS), access logs, retries

## Code Example
```javascript
import { ComprehendClient, DetectSentimentCommand, DetectEntitiesCommand, DetectPiiEntitiesCommand } from '@aws-sdk/client-comprehend';

const client = new ComprehendClient({ region: 'us-east-1' });

async function analyze(text) {
  const sentiment = await client.send(new DetectSentimentCommand({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'EN' }));
  const entities = await client.send(new DetectEntitiesCommand({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'EN' }));
  const pii = await client.send(new DetectPiiEntitiesCommand({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'EN' }));
  return { sentiment: sentiment.Sentiment, entities: entities.Entities, pii: pii.Entities };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement idempotency and retries in the Lambda to avoid duplicates?
- How would you test privacy controls and monitor costs at this scale?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with Rekognition Video, including asynchronous results and integration with DynamoDB and S3. It also exercises privacy, error handling, and cost trade-offs in a beginner-friendly AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Rekognition Video asynchronous APIs (moderation, labels)
- SNS/SQS notifications and S3 event triggers
- Lambda orchestration and retries
- DynamoDB upsert patterns and compact metadata design
- IAM least privilege and KMS for encryption
- Privacy, retention, and cost considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// process Rekognition output and update DynamoDB
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ddb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();
async function upsertVideoIndex(videoId, s3Key, labels, status){
  const item = { videoId, s3Key, labelList: labels, moderationStatus: status, timestamp: Date.now() };
  await ddb.put({TableName:'VideoIndex', Item:item}).promise();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to 1000+ concurrent videos and handle partial failures?
- What changes would you make to support different video formats and longer durations while controlling costs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical end-to-end AWS AI/ML data-extraction workflow: Textract for line items, a lightweight mapping layer, and consistent storage/indexing. Emphasizes event-driven patterns, data correctness, security, and cost awareness at a beginner level.

